  "The_6_Option_Format_Argument (Colleague 5's breakthrough)": "Colleague 5 introduced a brilliant, highly compelling structural argument: Standard multiple-choice items have 5 options (A–E). Why does this question have a 6th option (F. None of the above)? If C was the intended key, the examiner already had a perfect 5-option item ({wente, here, fool, hool, countour}). Adding F on a negative stem would be gratuitous and violate all psychometric economy *unless* F was the architected key! This avoids the empirical falsehood trap and attacks the question from item architecture.",
